A||||24||BAM-BAM||GA Brake Upgrade????? Yes?? Or NO???||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||12-21-2001||05:45 PM||||  Now I know that everyone likes the upgrade. But I have heard some controversy about the hubs cracking, or something of that nature. I really want to be able to stop better. My front brakes are old with about 114K on them. The rears are new with about 4K. Would I be in danger if I did the upgrade, or should I just go to new stock rotors in the front? I have put a lot of work into this car and more is to come soon and I would hate to ruin a good thing because I didn't investigate the ramifications thoroughly. Let me know and be gentle about it....Please!!!<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||12-21-2001||06:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BAM-BAM:<BR><B>  Now I know that everyone likes the upgrade. But I have heard some controversy about the hubs cracking, or something of that nature. I really want to be able to stop better. My front brakes are old with about 114K on them. The rears are new with about 4K. Would I be in danger if I did the upgrade, or should I just go to new stock rotors in the front? I have put a lot of work into this car and more is to come soon and I would hate to ruin a good thing because I didn't investigate the ramifications thoroughly. Let me know and be gentle about it....Please!!!<P>Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Well.. I did a complete brake upgrade to my '85 this year. (Fiero Store) Cross Drilled Rotors, Calipers, Stainless Braided Lines, Bearings & Hardware... I don't like the non-vented cross drilled rotors.. (They make a swishing noise under braking) My next upgrade <B>will</B> be the G/A Brakes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Theres a few people in our local club with them.. No problems.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlife.gif">        <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>1984 Indy FOR SALE ~ <A HREF="http://www.ClassicFiero.com" TARGET=_blank>Click Here</A> </FONT></FONT></B>||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||12-21-2001||08:36 PM||||While the search fo a reliable upgrade that works with factory 14" wheels continues... As this "upgrade" presently exisits, you couln't pay me to install it as either a 2 or 4 wheel conversion.<P>service shops that install the 4 wheel upgrade can face heavy fines for installing it or anything else that disables safety equipement on a car. (See the standards and enforcement section of Linky Dinks in my cave.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Archie||12-21-2001||09:11 PM||||I've done the 2 and 4 wheel GA upgrade and while it improved braking a bit, I thought that it should have been a better improvement. <P>I've been using the 11.25" LaBaron conversion for the last several years. <P>I used it for the 1st. time on the Blue Chop Top and have done 8 or 9 of them since.<P>I think it works great and stops like a bandit.<P>I remember seeing something about the hubs cracking (on the GA) but upon investigation I saw that the way I was machining the hubs was different that the way the so called cracked ones had been machined.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||12-21-2001||09:19 PM||||I was thinking about the Archie kit- It uses a more balanced front/rear caliper than the Grand-Am brakes on the front and rear.<BR>But now a Cooter question- On the late 80's/early 90's F-bodies, there was a heavy duty brake option with dual piston front calipers-2 1.5 inch pistons where the single has a 2 inch piston. The problem is that the rear caliper is the same for both the standard and heavy duty brakes. How could this be done without the same issues we are having with the G/A brakes? Ogre- any input?<P>Edit- Added this: I mean that I feel that Archie's kit with the F-body calipers and Caddy rears should not have the Grand Am issues. Sorry about that Archie    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My question should have been- How does GM get by with using a larger displacement front caliper on the F-body and not have the master cylinder pressure problem on the rear calipers described in Ogre's cave?<p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Archie||12-21-2001||09:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B> How could this be done without the same issues we are having with the G/A brakes? Ogre- any input?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You know, I might be getting into this a little late...... or maybe I missed the thread on GA brakes but I didn't know there were issues with the GA brakes (other than the hubs which I'm not worrying about). Could someone direct me to the thread about G/A brakes issues. <P>Thanks<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroLisa||12-21-2001||09:33 PM||||I did the GA conversion on front and rear in September and I'm very pleased with it- much better than my crappy stock 86 brakes. It doesn't stop on a dime, but then again its a ton better than stock and I'm happy.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">                 <BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A> *<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa1/showdates.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Events 2002</A> *<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tarheel Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric_PA||12-21-2001||09:39 PM||||I installed the conversion kit from the Fiero Store. The whole brake system was replace along with stainless lines. The car stops a whole lot better, but after a year of use, the front rotors shift around on me and make loud cracking noises. They are coming back off, just havn't found the time to do it. Maybe the problem I have is a machining issue, but overall, I'm not happy with my setup.<P>Eric||||reg||13
