Z||000010||jelly2m8||12-21-2001||09:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> You know, I might be getting into this a little late...... or maybe I missed the thread on GA brakes but I didn't know there were issues with the GA brakes (other than the hubs which I'm not worrying about). Could someone direct me to the thread about G/A brakes issues. <P>Thanks<P>Archie<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> The only real issue is some one going off on how unsafe, and illegal the GA brake conversion is, everytime someone asks about this. <P> I bet he hasn't even driven a GA brake equipped fiero, let alone done the conversion.<P> <P> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Joe Torma||12-21-2001||10:13 PM||||Hey, the new Grand Ams have disc brake in the rear with a similar cable setup for the E-Brake.<P>Looks like the rotors are larger too.  Anyone investigate this?<P>Archie, the hubs you machine...does it move the rim out further than stock?<P>Also, would it be "safe" to make the stock rotor into a "hat" with the rotors that bolt on?  i've been wondering this for a while.  I was thinking on machining off most of the rotor surface, drilling and tapping holes for wilwood rotors.  What do you think?<P><BR>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||MrPBody||12-21-2001||11:04 PM||||Aside from safety concerns, I vote "no" because the GA conversion:<P>&gt; Increases unsprung weight<P>&gt; Sets the wheels farther outboard, increasing front scrub radius -- of which '84-'87 already has too much.<P>I thought hard about doing this conversion on my '85, but installed <B>good</B> pads instead.  I'm quite happy with the way it stops.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||12-21-2001||11:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B> would it be "safe" to make the stock rotor into a "hat" with the rotors that bolt on?  i've been wondering this for a while.  I was thinking on machining off most of the rotor surface, drilling and tapping holes for wilwood rotors.  What do you think?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldn't do that..... the stock rotor is cast iron. Cast Iron doesn't like drill and tap like a regular machined part would.<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000014||BAM-BAM||12-22-2001||12:10 AM||||  Thanks everyone  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Looks like I'll go with new rotors and stainles steel lines until I can afford one of Archies kits. <P>  Hey jelly, lighten up man. Archie has done some pretty awesome things for the Fiero community and usually does his research first. Be cool bro!!<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||jelly2m8||12-22-2001||12:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BAM-BAM:<BR><B>  Thanks everyone   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Looks like I'll go with new rotors and stainles steel lines until I can afford one of Archies kits. <P>  Hey jelly, lighten up man. Archie has done some pretty awesome things for the Fiero community and usually does his research first. Be cool bro!!<P>Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Oh, I was not putting Archie down in anyway. I was pointing out something else.<P> I would love to have Archies big brakes, but they are out of my price range right now.<P> But my V8 is an Archie!<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||KissMySSFiero||12-22-2001||12:40 AM||||ive never driven a Pre88 car so i cant say much for the brakes(drove a rusted 84 about half a block)  but from what im told(looking for trouble there)  the GA upgrade is about the same as the 88 brakes.  personally im ready for bigger brakes for my 88(winter project)  the brake upgrade archie offers is awesome.  ive never driven a car with it, but i was on one when the driver laid into the pedel.  and it stopped way better than expected.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>Two 88FORMULAS Red & Yellow<BR>        <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>         <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fierobsessed||12-22-2001||11:49 AM||||I have done the GA Brake conversion on both of my cars. On my Indy, I only did it on the back. On my 85 2m4 3.1, I did it only in the front. My Indy will make anyone who is not wearing there seatbelt kiss the windshield. The 85 will just skid, and skid, and continue to skid. I only did the upgrade on the front brakes because the bleeder screws on the front calipers were both broken off and air was also in the line already when I got the car. I had the option of just getting new stock calipers for $$$ or I could get a complete and better (Grand Am)brake system for the front for $86. I have a lathe already so it wouldn't cost me anything to do the swap. So I did it. Yes they work good, but the rear brakes are not doing much, so my car skids forever, hell I can probably get the car from 0 to 40 with the front wheels locked. But the Indy is a different story. I only put the GA brakes in the back, It made alot of sence, my rear calipers were leaky and the E brake was useless anyway, Eithter I buy new rear calipers $$$$ or for 86$ upgrade the rear alot. Well this change made all the difference in the world. The stock front calipers are good enough to lock up the brakes with little effort, so I figured they are good enough, and I learned that my rear brakes did nothing, even though they worked ok. The rear brakes couldnt lock up in snow in neutral, I'm not kidding. So when I put the Grand Am brakes on, the rear brakes pinch much harder, causing the front end to dive down, making the front brakes more effective, plus the rear wheels helping the car stop, well it just stops on a dime.<P>------------------<BR>Matt D<BR>1984 Indy Fiero (auto)<BR>1985 2m4 V6 DIS 3.1 Auto||||reg||11
Z||000018||theogre||12-22-2001||02:20 PM||||Jelly, <BR>You couldn't pay me to drive a car with F'd up brakes either. Justify it however you want. It doesn't change the facts.<P>The 4 wheel GA upgrade is not street legal and fines for shops installing it Start at $1000 per violation, and per violation doesn't mean per car. They could count each part altered/disabled on a single car as an individual violation.<P>I started doing this conversion and when I saw the problems I couldn't beleive people would put cars with it on the street. People who are actually qualified to work on brakes and actually understand how car parts are suposed to fit together can easily see what is wrong. Many problems I've pointed out have been independantly verified by other forum members.<P>Joe, <BR>No, you can't just bolt a Wilwood style rotor to a cut off cast hub... As mentioned above, the cast likely won't play nice. Also, Wilwood's hubs and rotors fit together like Leggo blocks. The bolts mainly hold the 2 parts together. The "leggo fit" takes the rotational load durring stopping. Duplicating that fit would be allot of work.<P>Cooter,<BR>Without seeing the parts you mention, I can't begin to guess if they could be done on Fiero.<P>Archie,<BR>Two threads related to the issues of conversion would be<P><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016355.html" TARGET=_blank>Improved Front Brakes</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007762.html" TARGET=_blank>Brake Upgrade Math</A><BR>There are several others.<P>The upgrade math was the start, so to speak, of the upgrade article in my cave. Someone in there also covered some of the machine issues for the hub/rotor.<P>Others,<BR>There are also now multiple people who've said they dislike or want to remove the upgrade either due to fit or performance problems. (Including at least one in this thread..)<P>Grand Am calipers are considerably heavier. So are the rotors. When you are trying to build a car to handle one of the last things you want is to push up unsprung weight. I would not be suprised if using GA stuff doubles unsprung weight of the brakes.<P>Increased scrub radius, mentioned above, is also a problem. This affects handling. Fiero already has enough handling issues to work out without adding more.<P>Rotor fit continues to be a major problem. The only acceptable index point is the rotor center hole. The inside of the rotor hat is not uniform diameter or finish and cannot provide reliable centering. A hat centered hub that fits a particular rotor today very likely will not fit the next one the same way if at all.<P>Hub Cracking? Hadn't heard that one before. Would have to see the hub before I could say why.<P>Finally, <P>The majority of people comparing the "improvment" of GA vs. OE are NOT comparing apples to apples. They are comaring new parts against OE parts that are worn and probably had major problems. Such a comparison is totally meaningless.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||yo_sweet||12-22-2001||07:52 PM||||damn u guys rule , my fiero has front and rear drums they suck real bad i want disc brakes!<P>anyone got a setup in canada?<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fierobsessed||12-22-2001||08:25 PM||||I agree with most of the points made on this thread, but I must say, Fiero's brakes are adequate for a commuter car they stop the car in 187 feet with maximum load, and 183 with minimum load from 60. Using the E brake, 358 feet. (this is from my 84 owners manual) By todays standards, its awful. Now look at a modern production car with a simular configuration, say, the Mr2. The new Mr2 can stop from 60 in 92 feet, witch is (I think) the record for a production car. I do consider the E brake an essential, however not too many Fiero's have a working E brakes anyway. There was even a recall on all the 84 to 87 stick models of Fiero's and even firebirds, The parking brake was not considerd adequate so they were replaced on those models only. But the automatics went on neglected. The only solution to the biggest problem with the Grand Am upgrade would be to have an Auxiliary Mechanical Caliper as an E brake. As for the other concern, the added unsprung weight, well... it seems no matter how you upgrade your brakes you always end up increasing your unsprung weight. Its just a question of how much. The huge majority of the weight is added not really by the caliper, but by the rotor, they are heavy. Fiero's brakes are designed with only 2 objectives, low unsprung weight, and general functionality, after all, it is a commuter car, remember?<BR>After doing the front Grand Am upgrade myself I feel pretty good about how it all goes togather. Theres only 1 complaint I have, the wheel is pushed out alittle (3/8") and is no longer centerd on the hub. However I have NOT noticed any difference in wheel feedback, maybe its just my steering stabilizer doing its job. As for the fit, no complaints here. I just kept taking down the diameter .001" at a time till the Rotor snugly fit onto the hub. I trust the front brakes completley. The metal that the hub's/rotors are made out of is pretty tough. I had to use a carbide bit to cut with, and even then it was a struggle. The thickness of the metal where the studs pass through is actually pretty thick, about 5/8" as opposed to the steel on the back hub witch is about 5/16" thick. The only difference is that the front hubs are cast and the rear ones are stamped. Also, before I bothered to cut the rotor from the hub, I used a roller tipped micrometer to make sure the centering was spot on before I started cutting. but for the most part, the back of the hub is flush and the metal just around it is too. Now if only I could get a set of mechanical aux calipers before my inspection is due on Dec 2002. (spelling checker does not work on my computer so ignore any spelling errors)<P>------------------<BR>Matt D<BR>1984 Indy Fiero (auto)<BR>1985 2m4 V6 DIS 3.1 Auto||||reg||3
Z||000021||jelly2m8||12-22-2001||08:53 PM||||Ogre, thats fine.<P> You can do all the research you want, I still don't care what you have to say.<P> Everytime someone posts about the GA swap, you always have a response and the tread turns into a flame war. Now you have one going with MrFieroUK.<P> Will it ever end?.......<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||SCCA FIERO||12-23-2001||04:09 AM||||If you are comparing the old factory calipers/rotors to all new GA units, won't that be an improvement? What if you replaced the stock calipers/rotors with new/Reman units?<P>Aren't the GA rotors the same diameter as the stock Fiero? And I think the piston is just a little bigger then the piston in the Fiero caliper.<P>Other than the fact that the GA rotors are vented, I don't think it out weighs the fact that you lose your parking brake. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||12-23-2001||11:30 AM||||Sorry, but he was right about absolutely everything else... I'd listen to his opinion in the matter. The numbers add up. What's the problem?||||reg||12
Z||000024||theogre||12-23-2001||02:42 PM||||I'm such a flame magnet... Only becuase I won't sit quietly and ignore people pushing a so called upgrade with problems and little to no real data to back it up. So far the only person to provide any good data is MrFieroUK.... One other person has reported thermal test data as it relates to an autocross application. Stoping distance by itself is not a meaningful test.<P>Several people have now reported problems with installed sets, inlcuding the set from Fiero Store. Others are unimpressed with the "upgrade" performance even when it's not having problems.<P>If certain people actually read the complete improved front brakes thread..... There's nothing flame about it. One thing we are both trying to figure out is how such a crappy hand brake could be installed in the first place. <P>The fact is that the only standards for the brake we've see so far indicate the hand brake only has to hold the car, Not stop it. It takes much less force to hold the car than to stop it. This explains why the hand brake is such a piece of crap to start with.<P><BR>E brake recall... <BR>I told MrFieroUK to stop trying to certify the car until he can verify that those rear pistons are working. trying to certify the car with screwed up rear pistons will make life harder than it already is. <P>The original style "plug faced" rear piston is the subject of the recall on the manual transmission cars. I don't trust them much on the automatics. I suspect there are allot more of them weak or outright broken then working correctly.<P>The problem is actually inside the piston. Apparently the ball bearing in there was weak in the original style. It would fail and screw up the piston. The recall replaced the piston with one having better bearings but otherwise changed little. <P>I guess they figured the problem wasn't important in automatics becuase of the transmission's park function. I think they should have repared all of them.<P>Obsessed,<BR>Spot calipers are what Baer and Willwood use in many of their systems. This was one of the first things I looked into for the GA upgrade.<P>The main problem you've got is how to mount the things. There's enough room for one, just little/nothing to bolt it's bracket to. (You might also need differant cables but that's not the big item.)<P>Unlike the front, which is on a separate bracket, the rear caliper mount is cast as part of the rear hardware. If you push out the bearings with a bracket you'll have to mess with both the CV and the main caliper. <P>It might be posible but extremely dificult to do a spot caliper, especially w/o a whole new mount for the main caliper.<P>On manual specifications.... When I looked in my 87 manual I thougth it was interesting, and rather scarry, that when the booster fails that stoping distance is 302 feet, nearly as long for E brake only. (e brake is 334 for acording to 87 manual) Yikes.||||reg||1
